// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row self_call_in_return_kills_param_drops (tier 1). A
// SELF-RECURSIVE CALL IN RETURN POSITION SUPPRESSES THE FUNCTION'S PARAMETER
// DROPS ON EVERY PATH — INCLUDING THE PATH THAT NEVER TAKES IT.
//
// `fn inner(d: String, b: bool) { if b { return inner(d, false); } }` called with
// `b = false` does not recurse at all, falls straight through, and still never
// destroys `d`: 1 alloc / 0 frees, 22 bytes definitely lost, rc 0.
//
// TWO CONTROLS SEPARATE IT FROM EVERY WIDER CLAIM (both 1 alloc / 1 free, 0
// errors, same binary):
//   * NOT "a param moved on one branch": `fn inner(d: String, b: bool)
//     { if b { sink(d); } }` — a DIFFERENT callee in the same branch, correct.
//   * NOT "return position": `{ if b { return sink(d); } }` with a different
//     callee, correct.
//   * NOT "self-recursion": `{ if b { inner(d, false); } }` — the same self-call
//     NOT in return position, correct.
// The discriminator is exactly SELF-CALL + RETURN POSITION.
//
// ROOT, by symbol: `SemaChecker`'s function epilogue at src/compiler/sema_decl.cpp
// `emit_frame_drops(frame, epilogue_drops, &body_ever_moved_)`. `body_ever_moved_`
// is the conservative "a param moved on ANY branch gets no drop at all" skip
// list, and that call's own comment names drop-flag elaboration as the proper
// fix and its cost as exactly this leak. The two non-self controls above show
// the skip list is NOT populated for an ordinary branch move, so the self-call
// in return position is what puts `d` in it.
//
// Found by tests/lattice/valgrind/sweep.sh; corpus member
// tests/imported/pass/recursion/recursion-tail-call-no-arg-leak.logos — a
// fixture whose NAME says "no-arg-leak" and which leaks (1 alloc / 0 frees,
// 6 bytes definitely lost), green through run_test.sh for as long as it has
// existed. Rust: exit 0, no leak.
//
// ⚠ THE ORACLE IS A DESTRUCTOR COUNT: 1000 = the parameter was destroyed on
// the fall-through path, 0 = it was not. `b` is FALSE here, so the recursive
// call is never taken and the function does nothing but return. Today 0,
// exit 1; correct 1000, exit 0.
package self_call_in_return_kills_param_drops;

struct D { v: i64, c: *mut i64 }
impl Drop for D { fn drop(self: &mut D) { unsafe { *self.c = *self.c + self.v; } } }

fn inner(d: D, b: bool) {
    if b { return inner(d, false); }
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let p: *mut i64 = &mut n;
    inner(D { v: 1000i64, c: p }, false);
    let got: i64 = unsafe { n };
    if got != 1000i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
